WN.lb(35,{"mt__p2l0DVNL2":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"numeric","id":"m1","prompt":"In the wood there are {a} toadstools and {b} trees. How many are fungi?","answer":"a","hint":"Trees are plants. Toadstools are fungi.","explain":"Only the toadstools are fungi. Trees are plants. So {a} are fungi.","params":{"a":[2,9],"b":[2,9]},"where":"a!=b"},{"type":"numeric","id":"m2","prompt":"A bowl has {a} old oranges with mould and {b} fresh ones. How many have a fungus growing on them?","answer":"a","hint":"Mould is a fungus. Which oranges have mould?","explain":"The mould is a fungus, and it is only on the old oranges. So {a} oranges have a fungus.","params":{"a":[1,6],"b":[2,7]},"where":"a!=b"},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Is a mushroom a plant?","choices":["No, it is a fungus","Yes, it is a plant","No, it is an animal"],"answer":0,"hint":"Does a mushroom have green leaves?","explain":"A mushroom is a fungus, not a plant. It has no green leaves and does not make food from light."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Where will mould grow best?","choices":["On an old, damp piece of bread","On a clean, dry plate","On a stone"],"answer":0,"hint":"Mould needs food and a damp place.","explain":"Mould feeds on old food and likes it damp. A clean plate and a stone have nothing to feed on."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"What do fungi do to dead leaves?","choices":["Break them down into soil","Make them green again","Nothing"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about the log and the leaf in the lab.","explain":"Fungi feed on dead leaves and break them down. What is left becomes soil for new plants."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Ben says mould just comes from nowhere. What is right?","choices":["It grows from tiny spores in the air","Ben is right","The baker puts it in the bread"],"answer":0,"hint":"Spores are too small to see, but they float in the air.","explain":"Mould grows from tiny spores that float in the air and land on old food. They are too small to see."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Which one is a fungus?","choices":["Toadstool","Daisy","Pebble"],"answer":0,"hint":"One is a plant and one was never alive.","explain":"A toadstool is a fungus. A daisy is a plant, and a pebble was never alive."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Your bread has fuzzy mould on one corner. What should you do?","choices":["Do not eat it","Eat the other end","Scrape it off and eat it"],"answer":0,"hint":"Mould can spread through soft bread where you cannot see it.","explain":"Do not eat mouldy bread. The mould can spread inside the bread, even where you cannot see it."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Fuzzy green spots grow on old bread. What are they?","choices":["Mould, a fungus","Dirt","Tiny plants"],"answer":0,"hint":"The spots get bigger every day. Does dirt grow?","explain":"The spots are mould. Mould is a fungus. It is alive, so it grows and spreads."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"On the grass there are {a} mushrooms and {b} daisies. How many are fungi?","answer":"a","hint":"Daisies are plants. Count only the mushrooms.","explain":"Only the mushrooms are fungi. Daisies are plants. So {a} are fungi.","params":{"a":[2,8],"b":[2,8]},"where":"a!=b"},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"A log has {a} mushrooms on it. In the night, {b} more grow. How many mushrooms now?","answer":"a+b","hint":"Mushrooms are alive, so more can grow. Start at {a} and count on {b}.","explain":"{a} and {b} more make {=a+b}. Now there are {=a+b} mushrooms on the log.","params":{"a":[2,6],"b":[1,4]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"You see a big mushroom in the park. What should you do?","choices":["Look, and tell a grown-up","Taste a little bit","Pick it for dinner"],"answer":0,"hint":"Some wild mushrooms are poison.","explain":"Never eat a wild mushroom. Some are poison and can make you very ill. Look with your eyes and tell a grown-up."}],"explain":{"kid":["Leave some bread out for a week. Soon you see fuzzy spots. They are green, white or blue. The spots get bigger each day. That is mould. Mould is alive. It grows!","Mould is a fungus. Mushrooms and toadstools are fungi too. Fungi are not plants. They have no green leaves. They do not need light to make food.","Fungi eat old and dead things. They grow on old fruit, rotting logs and dead leaves. Bit by bit, the dead things break down. They turn into soil. So fungi help tidy up the woods.","Some mushrooms in the park or woods are poison. They can make you very ill. Look with your eyes. Tell a grown-up. Never eat a wild mushroom. Do not eat food with mould on it."],"adult":["Fungi are their own kingdom of living things, separate from plants and animals. Unlike plants, they have no chlorophyll and cannot make food by photosynthesis. They release enzymes onto dead material and absorb the nutrients, which is why they are called decomposers. Mushrooms and toadstools are the fruiting bodies of a much larger network of threads underground. Yeast, used to make bread rise, is a fungus too.","This topic is not a named statutory objective at ages 5 to 7, but it builds directly on them. England's Year 1 and Year 2 science asks children to tell apart things that are living, dead and never alive, and to explore micro-habitats such as under logs and leaf litter. It prepares for later work on decomposers (NGSS 5-LS2-1) and on sorting living things into broad groups, including fungi, in Year 6.","Common mistakes: thinking mould is dirt or just the food changing colour, rather than a living thing that grows; calling a mushroom a plant because it grows in the ground; thinking mould appears from nowhere, when it grows from spores, tiny cells that drift in the air; and believing that a mushroom which looks like a shop one is safe to eat. Some wild fungi, such as the death cap, can kill.","In the lab your child watches mould spots grow on bread over a week, sorts fungi from plants, taps the places where fungi grow, drags a fungus onto a dead leaf, a log and an apple core to watch them break down into soil, and sorts what is safe to do when they find a wild mushroom."]},"familyTip":"Put a slice of bread in a sealed clear bag with a few drops of water and look at it each day for a week, without opening it. Count the spots together. Then throw the bag away unopened. On a walk, look for mushrooms on logs and in leaves, but do not touch or pick them.","keyIdea":"Mushrooms, toadstools and mould are living things called fungi. One of them is a fungus. Fungi are not plants: they have no green leaves and do not make food from sunlight. They feed on old food, rotting wood and dead leaves, and break them down into soil. Mould grows from tiny spores that float in the air. Some wild mushrooms are poison, so look but never eat one you find.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Is it a fungus or a plant? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Fungus","Plant"],"items":[["Mushroom",0],["Daisy",1],["Mould on bread",0],["Oak tree",1],["Toadstool",0],["Grass",1],["Mould on an orange",0],["Sunflower",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagmould","caption":"Flagship lab: watch mould grow on bread, sort fungi from plants, find where fungi grow, watch fungi turn dead things into soil.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_EygMHKs8Ed":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"numeric","id":"m1","prompt":"Pondweed → tadpole → fish. A pond has {a} tadpoles and {b} fish. How many consumers is that?","answer":"a+b","hint":"Tadpoles and fish both eat. Animals that eat are consumers.","explain":"The tadpoles eat pondweed and the fish eat tadpoles, so all of them are consumers: {a} + {b} = {=a+b}. The pondweed is the producer.","params":{"a":[3,9],"b":[1,4]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m2","prompt":"A pond has {a} pondweed plants, {b} tadpoles and 2 fish. How many producers are there?","answer":"a","hint":"Producers make their own food. Which ones are plants?","explain":"Only the {a} pondweed plants make their own food, so there are {a} producers. The tadpoles and fish are consumers.","params":{"a":[2,8],"b":[3,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"What does every food chain start with?","choices":["A plant","The biggest animal","The Sun"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which living thing makes its own food?","explain":"A plant makes its own food, so it starts the chain. The Sun gives the plant light, but it is not a link in the chain."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Grass → rabbit → fox. All the rabbits go. What happens to the fox?","choices":["It has less food","Nothing changes","It starts to eat grass"],"answer":0,"hint":"What does the fox eat in this chain?","explain":"The fox eats rabbits. With no rabbits, the fox has less food. Foxes do not switch to eating grass."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Why is a rabbit a consumer?","choices":["It eats other living things","It is small","It lives in a field"],"answer":0,"hint":"Can a rabbit make its own food?","explain":"A rabbit cannot make its own food. It eats grass, which is a living thing, so it is a consumer."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Leaf → caterpillar → ? → hawk. What fills the gap?","choices":["Blue tit","Grass","Hawk"],"answer":0,"hint":"The gap needs something that eats caterpillars and is eaten by a hawk.","explain":"A blue tit eats caterpillars, and a hawk eats blue tits. Grass is a plant, so it goes first, not in the middle."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Where does a plant get its food?","choices":["It makes it in its leaves, using sunlight","It eats the soil","It drinks it from the rain"],"answer":0,"hint":"Plants are producers. What does produce mean?","explain":"A plant makes its own food in its leaves using sunlight, air and water. Soil gives it water, but soil is not its food."}]},"practice":[{"type":"numeric","id":"p1","prompt":"Grass → rabbit → fox. A field has {a} foxes and {b} rabbits. How many consumers is that?","answer":"a+b","hint":"Foxes and rabbits are both animals. Animals eat, so they are consumers.","explain":"Rabbits and foxes both eat other living things, so all of them are consumers: {a} + {b} = {=a+b}. Only the grass is a producer.","params":{"a":[1,5],"b":[2,9]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"A food chain has {a} living things in a row. How many arrows does it need?","answer":"a-1","hint":"There is one arrow in each gap between two living things. Count the gaps.","explain":"{a} living things in a row have {=a-1} gaps, so the chain needs {=a-1} arrows. Leaf → caterpillar → bird has 3 things and 2 arrows.","params":{"a":[3,6]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"A bird eats {a} caterpillars each day. How many caterpillars does it eat in {b} days?","answer":"a*b","hint":"Add {a} for each day, or work out {a} × {b}.","explain":"{a} caterpillars a day for {b} days is {a} × {b} = {=a*b}. The bird needs many caterpillars, so losing them would leave it hungry.","params":{"a":[2,5],"b":[2,4]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"A snail eats lettuce. Which is the right way to write it?","choices":["lettuce → snail","snail → lettuce","snail and lettuce"],"answer":0,"hint":"The arrow points from the food to the eater.","explain":"The lettuce is the food and the snail is the eater, so the arrow goes lettuce → snail."}],"explain":{"kid":["A rabbit eats grass. A fox eats the rabbit. We can write that as grass → rabbit → fox. This is a food chain. It shows who eats what.","The arrow means 'is eaten by'. It points from the food to the eater. Grass is eaten by the rabbit, so the arrow goes from the grass to the rabbit.","A food chain starts with a plant. A plant makes its own food from sunlight, air and water. So we call it a producer. Animals cannot make food. They eat plants or other animals. We call them consumers.","If one link goes, the rest of the chain feels it. With no rabbits, the fox has less food. And the grass grows tall, because nothing is eating it."],"adult":["The English national curriculum for Year 2 asks pupils to 'describe how animals obtain their food from plants and other animals, using the idea of a simple food chain, and identify and name different sources of food'. The terms producer and consumer are introduced alongside it, and Year 4 returns to food chains with predators and prey. In the US, NGSS 5-LS2-1 develops the same idea as a model of matter moving among plants, animals and decomposers.","A food chain is a model of feeding links. Green plants are producers: they make their own food by photosynthesis, using light, water and carbon dioxide from the air. Every animal is a consumer and must eat. The arrow shows the direction food, and so energy, moves: from the thing eaten to the thing that eats it. The Sun powers the chain but is not a link in it.","Common mistakes: drawing arrows from the eater to its food, as if the arrow meant 'eats'; starting a chain with a large animal or with the Sun; thinking plants get their food from the soil, when soil gives water and minerals but not food; calling a plant eater a producer because it eats plants; and believing that if one animal disappears, nothing else changes. Ask 'what does it eat?' and 'who eats it?' for each link.","In the lab your child builds three chains with the plant first, turns round arrows that point the wrong way, sorts six living things into producers and consumers, takes rabbits and then grass out of a chain to see who goes hungry, and fills the missing link in three longer chains."]},"familyTip":"At dinner, pick one food and make its chain: what did it eat, and what eats it? A chicken ate seeds: seeds → chicken → you. Draw the arrows pointing to the eater.","keyIdea":"A food chain shows who eats what. It always starts with a plant, because a plant makes its own food using sunlight. Animals cannot make food, so they eat plants or other animals. Each arrow points from the food to the eater: grass → rabbit → fox means the rabbit eats the grass and the fox eats the rabbit.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Is each living thing a producer or a consumer? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Producer: makes its own food","Consumer: eats other living things"],"items":[["Grass",0],["Rabbit",1],["Oak tree",0],["Fox",1],["Lettuce",0],["Snail",1],["Wheat",0],["Owl",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagchain","caption":"Flagship lab: build food chains plant first, turn arrows from food to eater, sort producers and consumers, take a link away, fill the gap.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_uVaS12lN1i":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Every food chain in a food web starts with…","choices":["a producer","the top predator","the biggest animal"],"answer":0,"hint":"What makes food from sunlight?","explain":"Chains start with a producer, a plant that makes its own food. Energy then passes to the animals that eat it."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Foxes eat rabbits and mice. If the rabbits vanished, what would most likely happen?","choices":["foxes would eat more mice","all the foxes would die at once","foxes would start eating grass"],"answer":0,"hint":"Does the fox have another food in the web?","explain":"The fox still has mice, so it eats more of them. The mice may then fall in number too."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"A deer eats only grass and leaves, but it is big. It is a…","choices":["primary consumer","secondary consumer","producer"],"answer":0,"hint":"The role depends on what it eats, not how big it is.","explain":"It eats only plants, so it is a primary consumer, whatever its size."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"What do decomposers such as fungi and worms do?","choices":["break down dead things into nutrients for the soil","make food from sunlight","hunt the top predators"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about what happens to a fallen leaf or a dead animal.","explain":"Decomposers break down dead plants and animals. The nutrients go into the soil, and plants take them up."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"An owl eats {a} mice and {b} voles each week. The voles disappear. If the owl still needs the same number of meals, how many mice must it eat each week?","answer":"a+b","hint":"Every vole meal must become a mouse meal.","explain":"{a} + {b} = {=a+b}. The owl now eats {=a+b} mice a week, so losing voles puts more pressure on the mice.","params":{"a":[4,9],"b":[2,6]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"A pond web has {a} kinds of water plant. Each is eaten by tadpoles, and a heron eats the tadpoles. Nothing else is in the web. How many food chains end at the heron?","answer":"a","hint":"Count one chain for each producer.","explain":"Each plant starts a chain: plant → tadpole → heron. That gives {a} chains ending at the heron.","params":{"a":[2,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"In a web, grass → grasshopper → frog → hawk and berries → mouse → hawk are two chains. Which living thing is in both?","choices":["the hawk","the frog","the grass"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look for a name that appears in each chain.","explain":"The hawk ends both chains. Shared species are what join chains into a web."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Sam says grass is a consumer because rabbits eat it. What is wrong?","choices":["grass makes its own food, so it is a producer","nothing, Sam is right","grass is a decomposer"],"answer":0,"hint":"Does grass eat anything, or make its own food?","explain":"Being eaten does not make something a consumer. Grass makes its own food from sunlight, so it is a producer."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"In a food web, an arrow goes from the grasshopper to the frog. What does it show?","choices":["the frog eats the grasshopper","the grasshopper eats the frog","the grasshopper is a producer"],"answer":0,"hint":"Arrows point from the food to the eater.","explain":"The arrow points to the eater, so the frog eats the grasshopper. Food and energy move from the grasshopper to the frog."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"Frogs eat grasshoppers. In a food web, a frog is a…","choices":["secondary consumer","primary consumer","producer"],"answer":0,"hint":"Does the frog eat plants or animals?","explain":"A frog eats an animal, the grasshopper, so it is a secondary consumer. Primary consumers eat plants; producers are plants."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"A snake eats {a} frogs and {b} mice each month. All the frogs disappear. If the snake still needs the same number of meals, how many mice must it eat each month?","answer":"a+b","hint":"Its frog meals must be replaced by mouse meals.","explain":"It needs {=a+b} meals: {a} that were frogs plus {b} mice. So it eats {=a+b} mice, and the mice are hit too. That is how a change spreads through a web.","params":{"a":[2,6],"b":[3,8]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"In a web, {a} different plants are each eaten by rabbits, and rabbits are eaten by foxes. Nothing else is in the web. How many food chains end at the fox?","answer":"a","hint":"Each plant starts its own chain: plant → rabbit → fox.","explain":"Each of the {a} plants starts one chain to the fox, so there are {a} chains. A web is many chains joined.","params":{"a":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"Only frogs eat grasshoppers in a meadow. If all the frogs went, what would happen to the grasshoppers?","choices":["they would increase","they would decrease","nothing would change"],"answer":0,"hint":"Who was eating the grasshoppers?","explain":"With nothing eating them, more grasshoppers survive, so their numbers go up. They might then eat more grass."}],"explain":{"kid":["A food web shows who eats what in a place. It is lots of food chains joined up. Each arrow points from the food to the eater. So mouse → snake means the snake eats the mouse.","Every chain starts with a producer. A producer is a plant. It makes its own food from sunlight. Animals that eat plants are primary consumers. Animals that eat other animals are secondary consumers.","To read a web, start at a plant and follow the arrows up. In a meadow, grass → grasshopper → frog → hawk is one chain. Berries → mouse → hawk is another. The hawk is in both.","Living things in a web depend on each other. If the frogs vanished, grasshoppers would go up, because nothing would eat them. Snakes and hawks would eat more mice. Decomposers, like fungi, break down dead plants and animals into food for the soil."],"adult":["Ages 8 to 9. This matches Year 4 Living things and their habitats in the National Curriculum in England (construct and interpret a variety of food chains, identifying producers, predators and prey; recognise that environments can change and that this can pose dangers to living things) and NGSS 5-LS2-1 (model the movement of matter among plants, animals, decomposers and the environment). It builds on Simple Food Chains.","A food web links the food chains in one habitat. Arrows show the direction food and energy move, from the organism eaten to the one that eats it. Producers make their own food by photosynthesis. Primary consumers eat producers, and secondary consumers eat other consumers. Decomposers break down dead matter and return nutrients to the soil, which plants then take up.","Common mistakes: reading arrows the wrong way, as 'eats' instead of 'is eaten by'. Calling an animal a producer because other animals feed on it. Deciding a role from size rather than diet. Thinking a predator dies out if one prey vanishes, when a web gives it other foods. And leaving decomposers out of the picture.","In the lab your child finds every animal that eats the mouse and gives six living things their role. They build two chains that end at the hawk and spot what the chains share. They take the frogs away, then the rabbits, and follow the effects along the arrows. Last, they pass a dead hawk to the fungi and send the nutrients back to the plants."]},"familyTip":"Pick an animal you saw today, such as a blackbird, and list what it eats and what might eat it. Draw arrows from food to eater, then ask: if one of those disappeared, who would go hungry and who would do well?","keyIdea":"A food web is many food chains joined together. Each arrow points from the food to the eater. Every chain starts with a producer, a plant that makes food from sunlight. Primary consumers eat plants, and secondary consumers eat other animals. Decomposers such as fungi break down dead things into nutrients for the soil. Because the chains are linked, removing one species changes the numbers of what it ate and of what ate it.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Producer or consumer? Drag each card into a box, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["producer (makes its own food)","consumer (eats other living things)"],"items":[["grass",0],["rabbit",1],["oak tree",0],["frog",1],["berry bush",0],["hawk",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagfoodweb","caption":"Flagship lab: find every eater of the mouse, label producers and consumers, trace two chains, take frogs and rabbits away, and recycle a dead hawk.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_Sa48W7KXB5":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"A stone is…","choices":["never alive","dead","alive now"],"answer":0,"hint":"Did a stone ever eat, grow or have babies?","explain":"A stone never ate, grew or had babies, so it was never alive. 'Dead' is only for things that were once alive."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"A tree has no leaves in winter. It is…","choices":["alive, resting","dead","never alive"],"answer":0,"hint":"What happens to the tree in spring?","explain":"The tree is resting in winter. In spring it grows new leaves, so it is alive."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"A wool hat came from a sheep. It is…","choices":["once alive","never alive","alive now"],"answer":0,"hint":"Where does wool grow? Is that animal alive?","explain":"Wool grew on a living sheep, so the hat is made from something once alive."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Which of these is alive now?","choices":["a seed in a packet","a bone","a metal spoon"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which one can still grow?","explain":"A seed is resting. Plant it and it grows, so it is alive. A bone was once alive. A spoon was never alive."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"Sam has {a} sticks and {b} metal keys. How many of his things were never alive?","answer":"b","hint":"Sticks come from trees. Metal comes from rocks.","explain":"The {b} metal keys were never alive. The {a} sticks came from a tree, so they were once alive.","params":{"a":[1,6],"b":[1,6]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"On a nature table there are {a} pine cones, {b} snail shells and {c} pebbles. How many things were never alive?","answer":"c","hint":"Pine cones came from trees. Shells were made by snails.","explain":"Pine cones and shells were once alive. Only the {c} pebbles were never alive.","params":{"a":[1,5],"b":[1,5],"c":[1,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"We leave a log and a stone outside for a long time. What happens?","choices":["the log rots, the stone stays the same","the stone rots, the log stays the same","both grow"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which one was once alive?","explain":"The log was once part of a tree, so it slowly rots. The stone was never alive and does not rot."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"A cloud moves across the sky. It is…","choices":["never alive","alive now","once alive"],"answer":0,"hint":"Moving is not enough. Does a cloud eat, grow or have babies?","explain":"A cloud is made of tiny drops of water. It moves, but it was never alive."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"A dry brown leaf has fallen off a tree. Which group is it in?","choices":["once alive","never alive","alive now"],"answer":0,"hint":"Was the leaf ever part of something living?","explain":"The leaf was part of a living tree, so it was alive once. Now it has fallen and dried, so it is dead."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"A wooden chair is…","choices":["once alive, because wood comes from trees","never alive, because people made it","alive now"],"answer":0,"hint":"Where does wood come from?","explain":"Wood comes from trees, and trees are alive. So a wooden chair was once alive, even though people made it."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"Mia finds {a} feathers and {b} pebbles. How many of her things were once alive?","answer":"a","hint":"Feathers come from birds. Pebbles are bits of rock.","explain":"The {a} feathers came off birds, so they were once alive. The {b} pebbles were never alive. So {a} things were once alive.","params":{"a":[1,6],"b":[1,6]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"In a box there are {a} dry leaves, {b} shells and {c} stones. How many things in the box were once alive?","answer":"a+b","hint":"Leaves came from plants and shells came from animals. Stones never lived.","explain":"Leaves and shells were once alive: {a} + {b} = {=a+b}. The {c} stones were never alive.","params":{"a":[1,5],"b":[1,4],"c":[1,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"A river moves and makes a noise. Is it alive?","choices":["no, it was never alive","yes, because it moves","it was once alive"],"answer":0,"hint":"Does a river eat, grow or have babies?","explain":"A river moves, but it does not eat, grow or have babies. Water was never alive."}],"explain":{"kid":["Things can be in three groups. Some are alive now, like a cat or a sunflower. Some were alive once, but are dead now, like a dry leaf. Some were never alive at all, like a stone.","Living things eat or drink. They grow. They can have babies. A stone never did any of these things, so it was never alive.","Some things are made from something that was alive. A wooden chair came from a tree. A wool hat came from a sheep. Paper came from trees too. So they were once alive. A metal spoon came from rocks, so it was never alive.","Be careful. A river and a cloud move, but they were never alive. A seed, a bare tree in winter and a sleeping hedgehog are still. But they are resting, not dead. Dead things that were once alive slowly rot. Stones never rot."],"adult":["Ages 6 to 7. This matches Year 2 Living things and their habitats in the National Curriculum in England (explore and compare the differences between things that are living, dead, and things that have never been alive). It builds on Living and not living and leads into habitats and how fossils form.","The three groups are about history, not appearance. Living things carry out life processes now; Year 2 children use eating or drinking, growing and reproducing as the test. Dead things carried out those processes once, including parts of organisms (bones, shells, feathers) and materials made from them (wood, paper, wool, cotton, leather). Never-alive things, such as rock, metal, glass, water and air, never did.","Common mistakes: thinking anything that moves is alive, so rivers, clouds, cars and fire count. Thinking anything still is dead, so seeds, bare winter trees and hibernating animals are called dead. Putting made objects such as a wooden chair or a wool jumper in 'never alive' because a person made them. And confusing 'dead' with 'never alive', so a stone is called dead.","In the lab your child sorts a cat, a stone, a dry leaf and a sunflower into three groups. They drag a chair, wool, paper and a metal spoon back to a tree, a sheep or rocks. They watch three months pass as a tree grows, a log rots and a stone stays the same. Then they sort resting things and parts of animals, and check the signs of life for a river, a cloud and a tadpole."]},"familyTip":"On a walk, collect a few things: a leaf, a twig, a pebble, a feather. At home, sort them into three piles: alive now, once alive and never alive. For each one ask, 'Did it ever eat, grow or have babies, or come from something that did?'","keyIdea":"Everything belongs to one of three groups. Living things are alive now: they eat or drink, grow and have babies. Dead things were alive once, like a fallen leaf, a bone, or wood and wool that came from a tree or a sheep. Things that were never alive, like stones, metal, water and clouds, never ate or grew. Moving is not the same as living, and resting is not the same as dead.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Was it ever alive? Drag each card into a box, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["was alive once (now dead)","never alive"],"items":[["a dry leaf",0],["a pebble",1],["a wooden spoon",0],["a metal key",1],["a feather",0],["a cloud",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagonce","caption":"Flagship lab: sort things into alive now, once alive and never alive, trace wood and wool back home, watch a log rot, and check the signs of life.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_YB0qF5KX9C":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"What is deforestation?","choices":["Cutting down large areas of forest","Planting new forests","Animals moving out of a forest"],"answer":0,"hint":"'De' at the start of a word often means taking away, as in 'defrost'.","explain":"Deforestation is clearing forests, often for farms or building. It destroys habitats."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"What does a carbon footprint measure?","choices":["How much carbon dioxide is added to the air by what you do","How big your feet are","How much rubbish you throw away"],"answer":0,"hint":"It is about a gas, not a real footprint.","explain":"It adds up the carbon dioxide made by your travel, heating, food and things you buy."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Which energy source is renewable?","choices":["Wind","Coal","Natural gas"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which one will never run out?","explain":"The wind keeps blowing however much we use it. Coal and gas are fossil fuels and will run out."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"A species has only 50 animals left in the wild. Which word fits best?","choices":["Endangered","Extinct","Renewable"],"answer":0,"hint":"Some are still alive, but very few.","explain":"Endangered means at risk of dying out. Extinct would mean none left at all."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Litter is dropped in a park far from the river. Why can it still harm the river?","choices":["Rain and wind can carry it into the water","It cannot, it stays in the park","Litter turns into fish food"],"answer":0,"hint":"Remember what the rain did in the lab.","explain":"Rain washes litter into drains and streams, and wind blows it about. It ends up polluting rivers and the sea."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"Volunteers plant {=r} trees every weekend to help a wood recover. How many trees do they plant in {=w} weekends?","answer":"r*w","hint":"Multiply trees per weekend by the number of weekends.","explain":"{=r} × {=w} = {=r*w} trees. Replanting and protecting habitats is conservation.","params":{"r":[6,12],"w":[3,8]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Which choice is the most sustainable?","choices":["Mending a broken toy instead of buying a new one","Buying a new toy every week","Throwing toys away when they get dirty"],"answer":0,"hint":"Sustainable means it could go on without using up the Earth.","explain":"Mending uses no new materials or energy to make a new toy, so it can go on for ever."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Why is coal non-renewable even though it came from plants?","choices":["It took millions of years to form, so it will run out","Plants are not alive","Coal grows back each year"],"answer":0,"hint":"How long does it take for new coal to form?","explain":"Coal formed from plants buried millions of years ago. We use it far faster than it can form."},{"type":"mc","id":"m9","prompt":"What is conservation?","choices":["Protecting habitats and the living things in them","Using up fossil fuels","Counting how many animals there are"],"answer":0,"hint":"In the lab, what happened when you planted the trees back?","explain":"Conservation means protecting and restoring habitats, like nature reserves and replanting woods."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m10","prompt":"A patch of rainforest had {=t} hundred trees. Loggers cut down {=c} hundred of them. How many hundred trees are left?","answer":"t-c","hint":"Take the trees cut down away from the trees there were.","explain":"{=t} − {=c} = {=t-c}, so {=t-c} hundred trees are left. Every tree lost is a lost home for animals.","params":{"t":[15,30],"c":[4,12]}}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Which place has the most biodiversity?","choices":["A hedge with 30 different kinds of plant and animal","A field with 5,000 wheat plants","A car park with one tree"],"answer":0,"hint":"Biodiversity counts different kinds, not how many of one kind.","explain":"The hedge has 30 different kinds. The wheat field has thousands of plants but only 1 kind."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"A wood had {=a} kinds of bird. After deforestation, {=b} kinds lost their homes and left. How many kinds of bird are left?","answer":"a-b","hint":"Take the kinds that left away from the kinds there were.","explain":"{=a} − {=b} = {=a-b}. Only {=a-b} kinds are left, so biodiversity has fallen.","params":{"a":[12,20],"b":[3,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Which of these is a fossil fuel?","choices":["Oil","Wind","Sunlight"],"answer":0,"hint":"Fossil fuels formed from things that lived millions of years ago.","explain":"Oil, coal and natural gas are fossil fuels. They are non-renewable because they took millions of years to form."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"A family's car trips add {=k} kg of carbon dioxide to the air each week. How many kg is that in 4 weeks?","answer":"k*4","hint":"Multiply the amount for one week by 4.","explain":"{=k} × 4 = {=k*4} kg. That is part of the family's carbon footprint.","params":{"k":[5,15]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"What does 'extinct' mean?","choices":["There are none left alive anywhere","There are only a few left","It has moved to a new habitat"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of the dodo or the dinosaurs.","explain":"Extinct means every last one has died, so that kind can never come back. 'Only a few left' is endangered."}],"explain":{"kid":["Biodiversity means how many different kinds of living thing live in a place. A wood with owls, bees, mushrooms and flowers has lots of biodiversity. A field of only wheat has very little, even if it has a million plants.","A habitat is the home where a plant or animal finds food and shelter. When people cut down forests, it is called deforestation. Animals lose their homes. If a kind of animal has only a few left, it is endangered. If every last one dies, it is extinct, and it can never come back.","Pollution is harmful stuff getting into the air, water or land. Litter dropped on the ground can be washed into rivers by rain and hurt fish. Coal, oil and gas are fossil fuels. They took millions of years to form, so they will run out. They are non-renewable. Sun, wind and moving water will never run out. They are renewable.","Burning fossil fuels puts carbon dioxide gas into the air, and that warms the planet. Your carbon footprint is how much carbon dioxide is made because of the things you do. Walking, cycling and saving energy make it smaller. Living in a way that does not use up the Earth is called sustainability. Protecting habitats is called conservation."],"adult":["Ages 8 to 11. This topic is about using the words for human impact precisely. Biodiversity is the variety of living things in a place. It counts different species, not individuals, so a big field of one crop has low biodiversity. WWF's Living Planet Report names habitat loss, including deforestation, as the biggest cause of wildlife decline. Pollution, overuse and climate change add to it.","Endangered means a species is at serious risk of dying out. Extinct means none are left anywhere; the dodo was last seen in the 1600s. Conservation protects or restores habitats and species, for example with nature reserves, replanting and breeding programmes.","Renewable sources, such as sunlight, wind and moving water, are replaced naturally as we use them. Fossil fuels formed from ancient living things over millions of years, so they are non-renewable. Burning them releases carbon dioxide, the main gas behind climate change. A carbon footprint is the total of these gases caused by what a person does, usually given in kilograms of carbon dioxide. Sustainability means meeting our needs without harming the future.","Common mistakes: counting lots of one plant as high biodiversity; thinking animals just move to the trees that are left; mixing up endangered and extinct; calling coal renewable because it came from plants; and reading 'carbon footprint' as a real footprint or as rubbish. The lab's travel figures use the UK government's 2023 factors: about 0.17 kg of carbon dioxide per km by car and 0.1 kg per person per km by local bus. So a 4 km school day is about 0.7 kg by car and 0.4 kg by bus."]},"familyTip":"Walk round your local park or garden and count how many different kinds of plant, bird and minibeast you can spot. Then count the kinds on a mown lawn. Which patch has more biodiversity? At home, talk about one trip each week you could walk or cycle instead of going by car.","keyIdea":"People change the places where plants and animals live. Habitat destruction, such as deforestation, and pollution take away homes and food, so biodiversity falls and some species become endangered or even extinct. Burning fossil fuels (coal, oil and gas) is non-renewable and adds carbon dioxide to the air; your carbon footprint measures how much your choices add. Conservation, renewable energy and sustainable choices help protect habitats for the future.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Does it harm habitats or help protect them? Drag each card to a box, or tap the card then a box.","opts":{"bins":["⚠️ Harms habitats","💚 Helps protect them"],"items":[["Cutting down a rainforest for farmland (deforestation)",0],["Plastic bags blowing into the sea (pollution)",0],["Burning coal to make electricity",0],["Protecting a wood as a nature reserve (conservation)",1],["Using wind turbines for electricity",1],["Walking to school instead of going by car",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagenvimp","caption":"Flagship lab: count kinds in a wood and a field, cut and replant trees, keep litter out of a river, sort energy sources, shrink a carbon footprint.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_cM8YS6NXqi":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Which four things does a habitat give an animal?","choices":["Food, water, shelter and air","Toys, food, a bed and a car","Only somewhere to sleep"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of what the pond gave the frog.","explain":"A habitat gives food, water, shelter and air. A bed alone is not enough."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Where does a woodlouse live best?","choices":["Under a log, where it is damp and dark","In a pond","On a sunny path"],"answer":0,"hint":"A woodlouse dries out easily.","explain":"A woodlouse dries out in the sun, so it suits the damp, dark place under a log."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"When a bee visits a flower, who gets help?","choices":["The bee and the flower","Only the bee","Only the flower"],"answer":0,"hint":"What does the bee drink? What does it carry away?","explain":"The bee gets nectar to eat, and the flower gets its pollen carried, so it can make seeds."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m4","prompt":"A squirrel buries {n} acorns. It forgets 2 of them, and they grow into oak trees. How many acorns did it remember?","answer":"n-2","hint":"Take the 2 forgotten acorns away from {n}.","explain":"{n} take away 2 is {=n-2} acorns remembered. The 2 forgotten ones grew into new trees.","params":{"n":[4,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"A garden has a hedge but no pond. Will frogs live there?","choices":["No, frogs need a pond","Yes, a hedge is enough","Yes, frogs live anywhere"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which need does a frog get from a pond?","explain":"A frog needs a pond for water, so a garden with only a hedge does not meet its needs."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Which habitat suits a crab?","choices":["A rocky seashore","A woodland","Under a log"],"answer":0,"hint":"A crab needs salty sea water.","explain":"A crab needs salt water and rocks to hide under, so the seashore suits it."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"What is shelter?","choices":["A safe place to hide, rest or keep warm","Something to eat","Something to drink"],"answer":0,"hint":"The frog used reeds and stones for this.","explain":"Shelter is a safe place to hide, rest or keep warm, like reeds for a frog."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m8","prompt":"A hedge has {n} birds' nests. Each nest has 2 birds. How many birds is that?","answer":"2*n","hint":"Count in twos, one two for each nest.","explain":"{n} nests of 2 birds is {=2*n} birds. The hedge gives them all shelter.","params":{"n":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m9","prompt":"Which plant needs a pond habitat?","choices":["A water lily","An oak tree","A daisy on the lawn"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which one grows in water?","explain":"A water lily grows in water, so a pond is its habitat. Plants need suitable habitats too."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"What is a habitat?","choices":["A place where a living thing lives and gets what it needs","Just a place to sleep","A kind of animal"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of the pond. What did it give the frog?","explain":"A habitat is where a living thing lives. It gives it food, water, shelter and air."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"Why can a fish not live in a wood, even with lots of food?","choices":["It needs water to breathe","It does not like trees","It is too dark"],"answer":0,"hint":"Food is only one need. How does a fish breathe?","explain":"A fish takes air from the water, so out of water it cannot breathe. Every need must be met."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"A pond has {n} frogs. {=n+2} more frogs come to the pond. How many frogs are there now?","answer":"2*n+2","hint":"Add the frogs that came to the {n} already there.","explain":"{n} frogs and {=n+2} more make {=2*n+2} frogs. The pond gives them all food, water and shelter.","params":{"n":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"What do a duck's webbed feet suit it to?","choices":["Swimming on a pond","Climbing trees","Digging in soil"],"answer":0,"hint":"Webbed feet push water like paddles.","explain":"Webbed feet push the water, so a duck is suited to life on a pond."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p5","prompt":"A bee visits {n} flowers in the morning and 3 more in the afternoon. How many flowers did it visit?","answer":"n+3","hint":"Start at {n} and count on 3.","explain":"{n} flowers and 3 more is {=n+3} flowers. Each one gave nectar and got pollen.","params":{"n":[4,9]}}],"explain":{"kid":["A habitat is where a living thing lives. It is more than a home. It gives the living thing all it needs: food, water, shelter and air. Shelter is a safe place to hide, rest or keep warm.","A pond is a habitat for a frog. The frog catches flies to eat. It has water to swim in and keep its skin damp. It hides from herons among the reeds and stones. It breathes the air.","Most living things live in a habitat that suits them. A fish needs water to breathe, so it could not live in a wood, even with lots of food. A duck has webbed feet for swimming on a pond. A crab has a hard shell to keep it safe on a rocky shore. A woodlouse dries out in the sun, so it lives under a damp log.","Living things in a habitat need each other. A bee drinks nectar from a flower. As it does, it carries pollen to the next flower, so the flower can make seeds. A squirrel eats acorns from an oak tree. Some acorns it buries and forgets grow into new oak trees. Plants need their habitat too: a water lily needs a pond, and seaweed needs the sea."],"adult":["Ages 6 to 8. This matches the National Curriculum in England for Year 2, living things and their habitats: identify that most living things live in habitats to which they are suited, and describe how different habitats provide for the basic needs of different kinds of animals and plants, and how they depend on each other. It also fits NGSS K-ESS3-1 and 2-LS4-1. Key words: habitat, micro-habitat, basic needs, food, water, shelter, air, suited, depend, pollen, nectar.","The statutory guidance asks children to look at local habitats, such as a pond, woodland, hedge or seashore, and at micro-habitats such as under a log. Basic needs here are food, water, shelter and air (oxygen), plus light and a suitable temperature for plants. A fish takes oxygen from the water with its gills, so taking it out of water stops it breathing, not just drinking. Seashore animals such as shore crabs need salt water.","Common mistakes: thinking a habitat is only a home or a bed, not the place that meets every need. Thinking food alone is enough, so a fish could live anywhere if fed. Thinking any animal could live anywhere if it tried. Seeing only one side of a link, such as 'bees use flowers', when the flower is helped too. The lab, practice and challenge target each of these.","In the lab your child matches four things in a pond to the frog's four needs. Then they move a squirrel, a fish, a crab and a woodlouse between habitats, while a checklist shows which needs are met. Next they match webbed feet, spines, a hard shell and a damp-loving body to a habitat. They pair a bee, a squirrel and a blackbird with the plant each one helps. Last, they turn a lawn of short grass into a garden that a bee, a frog, a hedgehog and a blackbird move into."]},"familyTip":"On a walk, stop at one small habitat, such as a hedge, a pond or a pile of logs. Ask four questions together: Where could an animal find food here? Water? Somewhere to hide? Who lives here? Then lift a log or look under a leaf, gently, and put everything back as you found it.","keyIdea":"A habitat is the place where a living thing lives and gets everything it needs: food, water, shelter and air. A pond gives a frog flies to eat, water, reeds to hide in and air. Most living things live in a habitat they are suited to: a fish needs water to breathe, so it cannot live in a wood, and a duck's webbed feet suit a pond. Living things in a habitat also depend on each other: bees get nectar from flowers, and flowers get their pollen carried so they can make seeds.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Which habitat suits it best? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Pond","Woodland","Seashore"],"items":[["Frog",0],["Duck",0],["Squirrel",1],["Owl",1],["Crab",2],["Limpet",2],["Water lily",0],["Oak tree",1],["Seaweed",2]]}},"lab":{"type":"flaghabnd","caption":"Flagship lab: match what a pond gives a frog to each need, test habitats with a needs checklist, match body parts, pair helpers, build a garden.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_brgde1Vx0P":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"numeric","id":"m1","prompt":"{a} minibeasts go into a key. The first question is 'Does it have legs?' and {b} of them have legs. How many go down the no branch?","answer":"a-b","hint":"The minibeasts without legs take the no branch.","explain":"{b} of the {a} have legs, so {a} − {b} = {=a-b} have no legs and go down the no branch.","params":{"a":[6,12],"b":[1,5]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m2","prompt":"A key names {a} different trees by their leaves. How many yes or no questions does it need?","answer":"a-1","hint":"Each question makes one more group. How many splits until there are {a} groups?","explain":"Each question splits one group into two. Going from 1 group to {a} takes {a} − 1 = {=a-1} questions.","params":{"a":[4,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"A key asks 'Does it have 6 legs?' about a spider. Which way does it go?","choices":["No, a spider has 8 legs","Yes, spiders are insects","It does not fit the key"],"answer":0,"hint":"Count a spider's legs. Is a spider an insect?","explain":"A spider has 8 legs, so the answer is no. Spiders are not insects: insects have 6 legs."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Why is 'Does it have legs and wings?' a poor key question?","choices":["It asks two things at once","It is too short","No animal has wings"],"answer":0,"hint":"What would you answer for an animal with legs but no wings?","explain":"It asks about legs and wings together. An animal with legs but no wings has no clear answer, so ask one thing at a time."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Bee, butterfly, fish, frog. Which question splits them into two groups?","choices":["Does it have wings?","Is it alive?","Is it nice?"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which question gets some yes answers and some no answers?","explain":"The bee and butterfly have wings; the fish and frog do not. All four are alive, so that does not split them, and nice is an opinion."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"A leaf key asks 'Is the leaf in one piece?' An ash leaf has many leaflets along a stalk. What is the answer?","choices":["No","Yes","It depends on the size"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is an ash leaf one piece, or many small leaflets?","explain":"An ash leaf is made of many leaflets, so it is not in one piece. The answer is no."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"What does a classification key help you do?","choices":["Name a living thing by answering questions","Count how many animals there are","Show who eats what"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about what you have at the end of the key.","explain":"A key leads you, one yes or no answer at a time, to the name of a living thing. Showing who eats what is a food chain."}]},"practice":[{"type":"numeric","id":"p1","prompt":"A yes or no key names {a} different animals. Each question splits one group in two. How many questions does the key need?","answer":"a-1","hint":"Each question makes one more group. Start with 1 group and count up to {a}.","explain":"You start with 1 group. Each question adds one group, so reaching {a} groups takes {a} − 1 = {=a-1} questions.","params":{"a":[3,8]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"{a} animals go into a key. It asks 'Does it have wings?' and {b} say yes. How many go down the no branch?","answer":"a-b","hint":"Every animal goes down one branch: yes or no.","explain":"All {a} animals answer, and {b} say yes, so {a} − {b} = {=a-b} go down the no branch.","params":{"a":[6,10],"b":[1,5]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"You start with 1 group and ask {a} yes or no questions. Each question splits one group in two. How many groups do you end with?","answer":"a+1","hint":"Each split turns one group into two, so you gain one group each time.","explain":"Each question adds one group: 1 + {a} = {=a+1} groups at the end.","params":{"a":[1,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Which is a good question for a key?","choices":["Does it have a shell?","Is it lovely?","Is it quite small?"],"answer":0,"hint":"Can everyone look and give the same answer?","explain":"Anyone can check for a shell, so everyone gives the same answer. Lovely is an opinion, and quite small is vague."}],"explain":{"kid":["You find a small animal in the garden. What is it? A key can help. A key is a list of yes or no questions. You answer one, then follow the branch to the next.","Say the animal has legs. The next question asks, 'Does it have 6 legs?' A spider has 8 legs, so the answer is no. The key says it is a spider.","Good key questions are about things you can see or count. 'Is it cute?' is not a good question, because people do not agree. 'Does it have wings?' is a good one.","Each question splits a group into two smaller groups: the yes group and the no group. Keep splitting until each living thing is on its own. Keys work for plants and leaves too."],"adult":["The English national curriculum for Year 4 asks pupils to 'recognise that living things can be grouped in a variety of ways' and to 'explore and use classification keys to help group, identify and name a variety of living things in their local and wider environment'. Year 6 builds on this when pupils classify living things by observable characteristics and give reasons. The yes or no format is called a dichotomous key.","Each question tests one observable feature and splits the current group into two. Following answers from the first question narrows the group until one name remains. A key for n living things needs n − 1 questions, because each question makes one extra group. Keys can be drawn as branching diagrams or written as numbered pairs of statements.","Common mistakes: asking opinion or vague questions such as 'Is it pretty?' or 'Is it big?'; asking two things at once, like legs and wings together; choosing a question every member answers the same way, so nothing splits; calling a spider an insect; and skipping a question to jump to the name. Be careful to answer only the question in front of you.","In the lab your child follows a minibeast key for a spider, snail, ladybird and worm, judges five possible key questions, picks the question that splits three animal groups, builds a three-question key for a bird, bat, fish and crab, and uses a leaf key to name oak, ash, holly and horse chestnut leaves."]},"familyTip":"On a walk, collect four leaves or spot four animals. Take turns inventing one yes or no question that splits them into two groups, then another for each group, until every one has its own name.","keyIdea":"A classification key is a set of yes or no questions that helps you name a living thing. Each question splits a group in two, so you follow one branch at a time until only one name is left. Good key questions ask about one feature that anyone can see, count or check.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"The key asks: 'Does it have 6 legs?' Drag each animal into the yes or no box, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Yes: 6 legs","No"],"items":[["Ladybird",0],["Spider",1],["Ant",0],["Snail",1],["Bee",0],["Worm",1],["Beetle",0],["Woodlouse",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagkey","caption":"Flagship lab: follow a minibeast key, judge key questions, split groups, build your own key, and name leaves with a leaf key.","opts":{"step":0}}}});
